<section class="panel">
    <h2>Controller-Level + Action-Level Route Combination</h2>
    <p>
        A controller prefix combines with an action template. This exact concept is documented
        in the controller comments without adding a second live controller:
    </p>
<pre><code>[Route("society")]
public class ExampleController : Controller
{
    [HttpGet("complaints/{id:int}")]
    public IActionResult Details(int id)
    {
        ...
    }
}

Final URL: /society/complaints/5</code></pre>
    <p>
        The live controller does not use a controller-wide prefix because it intentionally
        demonstrates conventional and attribute routing together.
    </p>
</section>

<section class="panel warning">
    <h2>Constraint Is Not Validation</h2>
    <p>
        A route constraint decides whether a URL matches an endpoint. Business validation still
        belongs inside the action or service. The tracking action demonstrates both steps.
    </p>
</section>
